3.24.m:
Mārkaṇḍeya said:
One should worship the fourfold form of the Lord, which grants all desires;
a man desiring dharma should always worship Aniruddha.

3.25.m:
Praising the Lotus-eyed at Puṣkara, the Mace-bearer at Gayā,
and likewise Viṣṇu at Lohadaṇḍa, one crosses beyond misdeeds.
Praising me as Raghava at Citrakūṭa, as the Slayer of Daityas at Prabhāsa,
and as Govinda at Vṛndāvana, one becomes an inheritor of merit.
Praising Jaya at Jayantī, and Jayanta at Hastināpura,
Varāha at Kandamāla, and the Discus-bearer in Kaśmīra;
as Janārdana at Kubjāmra, and Keśava at Mathurā,
likewise Śrīdhara at Śrīkubja, and the Foremost of Gods at Gaṅgādvāra;
as Mahāyogya at Śāligrāma, Hari on Mount Govardhana,
the Four-armed at Piṇḍāraka, and the Conch-bearer at Śaṅkhadvāra;
as Vāmana at Kurukṣetra, and Trivikrama on the Yamunā,
likewise Viśveśvara on the Śoṇa, and Kapila at the eastern ocean;
and the Lord of Śvetadvīpa at the confluence of the Gaṅgā and the ocean,
the Bearer of the Earth on the bank of the Devikā, Yogaśāyin at Prayāga;

named Nara and Nārāyaṇa at the hermitage of Badarī,
and by the name Padmanābha at the southern ocean, O Arjuna;
likewise praising Kṛṣṇa at Dvārakā, one crosses beyond misfortune;
in this way, [praising] Rāma on Mount Mahendra, and likewise Hṛṣīkeśa, O wise one;
Hayagrīva at Aśvatīrtha, the Universal Form on the Himālaya,
Nṛsiṃha at Kṛtaśauca, and the Friend of the Twice-born on the Vipāśā;

3.27.m:
Having properly fasted on the third day of the bright fortnight of Jyeṣṭha,
a man should perform the worship of the earth with incense, garlands, and unguents,
with lamps and diverse foods, and he should give a sacrificial fee to brahmins:
vessels filled with seeds, along with gold, according to his means.
Thus the worship of the intermediate space is prescribed in Āṣāḍha;
a sacrificial fee of a pair of garments should be given there to a twice-born.
And the worship of heaven should be done in Śrāvaṇa just as before;
and there he should bestow as a sacrificial fee an umbrella and a pair of sandals.
Thus, in three months, the first breaking of the fast takes place here;
and another in three months, and again after that in three months;
then another in three months: in a year, O sinless one,
a set of four breakings of fast is to be understood for one constant in dharma.
Having performed the first breaking of the fast, he obtains the fruit of the Agniṣṭoma;
likewise, O king, at the second breaking of the fast, the fruit of the Atirātra.
At the third breaking of the fast, the wise man obtains the fruit of the Vājapeya;
at the fourth breaking of the fast, that which is declared the fruit of the Rājasūya.
